Celtic officials have announced the termination of Wilfried Nancy's contract after just eight matches in charge, according to an official club statement released on Monday. According to match records reviewed by multiple independent sources, Nancy, who arrived from Columbus Crew in early December, oversaw six defeats in those eight games. Sources close to the board confirmed that the move marks the shortest managerial reign in the club's modern history.
The decision follows an interim period under Martin O'Neill, who, according to performance data, had steadied the side with seven wins from eight matches, including victories over Rangers and Feyenoord. Analysts have noted the contrast in outcomes, with O'Neill's tenure featuring a League Cup semi-final success and a measurable reduction in the gap to league leaders Hearts; however, supporters contend that such short-term improvements must be weighed against longer-term institutional goals.
Nancy's first match in charge saw Celtic, deploying a 3-4-3 formation, suffer a 2-1 defeat to Hearts despite a bright opening, according to official match reports. Observers pointed to the tactical shift as a point of discussion, alongside other details such as the manager's choice of footwear and use of a tactics board on the sideline. Nancy himself stated post-match that he was "pleased with the mentality" and that "defensively, we were really good," though opportunities were missed, as he acknowledged.
Four days later, Roma secured a comfortable victory in the Europa League, with player Evan Ferguson later commenting that Celtic's players "didn't know what they were doing" at times, according to reports from multiple outlets. This result meant Nancy became the first Celtic manager to lose his opening two games, data from club archives indicates; on the other hand, club executives maintained that early results alone do not define a full evaluation process.
The Scottish League Cup final against St Mirren then offered an early chance for silverware. However, St Mirren claimed a 3-1 win, their first trophy in over a decade. Supporters and commentators expressed varying views in the build-up, with some suggesting the interim manager might have been better placed for the occasion, while club executives asserted their support for the appointment. On the pitch, Celtic appeared disjointed at times, with players in unusual positions, though Nancy expressed belief that the team would "click and move forward," as he stated publicly.
In the wake of the defeat, chief executive Michael Nicholson publicly stated that the board's support for Nancy was "absolutely solid," adding that it was the job of all involved "step by step" to back the manager and squad. Critics, however, argued the performances justified growing discontent among fans, according to fan surveys and media analysis; both perspectives were presented as legitimate positions in an ongoing institutional review.
Subsequent results included a 2-1 loss to Dundee United, followed by a dominant 3-1 home win over Aberdeen (73% possession and 31 shots, per official statistics) and a 4-2 victory against Livingston. A 2-0 defeat to Motherwell drew further scrutiny, with fan ratings for Celtic players remaining low according to BBC data; analysts suggest these mixed outcomes highlight the complexities of rapid managerial transitions.
The tenure concluded with a 3-1 Old Firm defeat to Rangers. Celtic took the lead, but Rangers responded strongly, according to match statistics. Some fans staged a protest after the final whistle, while others left early. Club officials have now acted, terminating the contract with immediate effect and reportedly preparing to reinstate Martin O'Neill on an interim basis until the end of the season, following established procedural guidelines.
Supporters and analysts continue to debate the rapid change, with some highlighting the challenging nature of the Glasgow football environment and others emphasizing the need for stability and results. The club has indicated a further update will be provided in due course, according to procedural statements. Officials from both sides of the discussion described the process as evidence-based and aligned with the club's long-term institutional objectives.
